From e7b3628d883177b7d3588f1d4574ae284f92abc5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 23:36:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09288/11362] Point the scored path at NVFP4 + a
 Blackwell-targeted llama.cpp build

THE ROOT CAUSE, which nothing was watching. The shipped llama.cpp was configured
CMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES=86 -- the dev 3090s -- and then sent to an
NvidiaRtxPro6000 (Blackwell, sm_120). Nothing in the project tied the build
target to the scored hardware, so the mismatch survived every hardware move. The
v22 log shows the generator does load on GPU, so this was not the CPU-fallback
disaster it looked like, but no NVFP4 kernel could ever have run there.

Rebuilt for "86;120": locally testable on the 3090s AND Blackwell-capable.
Verified sm_120 and sm_86 in the packaged libggml-cuda.so.0, and smoke-tested
the binary end to end on a 3090 -- server healthy, weights on GPU, /v1/chat
answering. Packaged self-contained (the CUDA runtime resolves from the package
directory, not the host) as carnot-llamacpp-blackwell-nvfp4, left alongside the
working CUDA-12 dataset rather than replacing it, so v22 stays reproducible.

KNOWN RISK, deliberately taken. The new build links libcudart.so.13 while the
previous bundle shipped CUDA 12, and Kaggle's driver version is not observable
from here -- it appears in no log we have. This is a bet on the save-run, NOT on
the submission slot: the save-run validates before any competition submit, so
being wrong costs wall-clock and a rebuild against a 12.8 toolkit, not the daily
slot.

MTP SCORED DEFAULT "0" -> "1". The "0" was correct for exactly one reason, that
Qwen3.8-27B had no published draft head, and that reason is gone: the NVFP4
build is self-drafting. Both halves now read True, so the v19 claims-versus-
resolves disagreement does not return. Verify `adding speculative implementation
'draft-mtp'` in the save-run log before trusting the speedup -- tok/s is
otherwise the only tell, which is the trap this area keeps setting.

TWO ORPHANED TESTS FIXED, both pre-existing and both failing at HEAD since the
2026-08-16 gemma migration -- confirmed by running them against a stashed tree
before touching either. One demanded the gemma MTP-head dataset be attached, a
configuration nothing has shipped for a day; the other asserted "31B" in a
repo_substr that now reads Qwen3.8-27B. Neither was caused by this change and
both are now asserted against the invariant rather than the model string: the
weights must be able to supply MTP by whatever mechanism, and the default must
track the single pinned constant. The next generator move costs one constant
instead of a fresh orphan.

31 tests green across the two files.

